diums shark Kage's political  conventions the Apollo trainings and  launches and protests it was and always  has been a handy tool to capture tight  quarters as well as huge spaces but  perhaps its greatest strength was making  rock stars appear larger than life when  the Beatles kicked off the British  Invasion in the mid 1960s the fish  Islands was uniquely suited to document  the insanity here they are at a Miami  press conference and during a TV  performance  the lenses warped perspective reflected  the trip eNOS of the psychedelic era  including at the Woodstock Music  Festival  [Music]  this is jazz musician Sam reverse 1965  album fuchsia swing song and it's one of  the earliest fisheye album covers a few  months later The Byrds released mr.  Tambourine Man over the next few years  fisheye album art cemented itself in  music the most common format was this a  giant circular fisheye lens portrait of  the artist or band with typography above  and below it and more Illustrated  approaches the font wraps fully around  the image and some exclude the artist  altogether opting to show an interesting  scene or landscape oh I just thought of  a fisheye album cover do you know we're  just read about Mike Oldfield no but I'm  gonna look it up right now oh wow that's  really cool the fisheye lens was a go-to  piece of gear for music photographers  and filmmakers by the end of the 1970s  it was there for concerts television  performances in photo shoots so by the  time MTV launched in 1981 it was pretty  much inevitable that the super wide  angle lens would play a huge role in  music videos  it could get super close to a performer  while still capturing the space around  them this is a music video for the  Beastie Boys track shake your rump it  was shot with at least three cameras  equipped with a fisheye lens the fisheye  worked for every genre from 60s hippies  to alt rock but hip-hop had a sense of  humor and it was in your face which  helped turn the fisheye into one of the  genres signature looks and in the 90s  that's thanks to director Mike Williams  from 1991 to 1999 he directed dozens of  music videos for top artists and many of  those prominently featured a fisheye  perspective Busta Rhymes TLC and of  course Missy Elliott the list goes on  and on and let's not forget those  fisheye album covers the fisheye lens is  no doubt a novelty when you see a photo  taken with it your eye will likely first  focus on the highly distorted style of  the image and then register what's in it  but that under sells how timeless and  useful of a tool it actually is one  generation might see it as a psychedelic  photography trope another a 1990s  hip-hop throwback if you're an  astronomer it helps you track clouds  moving across the atmosphere and the  terrain on the surface of Mars  and if you're as curious as Robert would  it'll let you see the world from the  point of view of a fish  [Music]
 

